/*
Key idea:

For an undirected graph, between two vertices u and v:
- there is exactly one simple path iff u and v are in the same connected component
  and every edge on the path is a bridge, equivalently u and v are in the same tree
  of the "bridge forest".
- there are at least two simple paths iff u and v are in the same connected component
  and their path in the bridge forest contains at least one 2-edge-connected block edge,
  equivalently after contracting each maximal subgraph with no bridges, u and v are in
  the same connected component but in different contracted vertices.

Thus the problem becomes:

Can we partition vertices into "blocks" (2-edge-connected components after removing bridges)
so that:
1) For every clue C=0, A and B must be in different blocks, but connected in the forest.
   Since every edge of the forest is a bridge, the relation "C=0" must be transitive on paths:
   all C=0 clues define edges in a forest over blocks.
2) For every clue C=1, A and B must be in the same connected component of that forest,
   but in different blocks.

A standard way:
- First, all pairs with C=1 can be inside same block unless forbidden by some C=0.
- C=0 means the endpoints must be in different block-nodes, and moreover all such relations
  must be realizable as a forest. In a forest, for every connected component, the pairs that
  must have unique paths are exactly pairs of distinct vertices in a tree. Therefore among the
  original vertices, relation C=0 must be an equivalence relation on connectivity? Not directly.

Better formulation using signed constraints on whether two vertices are in the same block:
- C=1 => they cannot be connected by only bridges, so if they are connected, there must be a cycle.
This is equivalent to saying in the final bridge-tree, their contracted nodes are distinct but in same tree.
This is hard directly.

Crucial observation:
Any connected component of an undirected graph can be represented as a tree of blocks.
For original vertices, define relation "~" : in the same block.
Then:
- C=0 iff A and B are in different ~-classes, and the ~-classes of A and B lie in the same tree.
- C=1 iff either same ~-class, or different ~-classes in same tree with distance >=1 and at least one bridge on path.
Wait "two or more simple paths" between vertices in a bridge-tree-of-blocks:
  if same block => yes
  if different blocks => every inter-block edge is bridge, so all simple paths must use the unique tree path,
     hence exactly one simple path overall. So C=1 iff same block.
This is the decisive simplification.

Because after contracting every non-bridge part into a block, the remaining graph is a forest.
Between different blocks in a forest there is exactly one simple path. Between vertices in same block
(2-edge-connected component), there are at least two simple paths.

Therefore:
- C=1 means A and B must be in the same block.
- C=0 means A and B must be in different blocks.

Then a graph exists iff these equality/inequality constraints are consistent.
And any consistent partition can be realized: make each block a cycle (or single vertex), and connect
blocks by bridges in a forest. Since there is no requirement for disconnected pairs, we can simply connect
all blocks in a tree; then any two vertices in different blocks have exactly one simple path, any two in the
same nontrivial block have at least two. For singleton blocks, no C=1 can occur inside them.

Construction existence reduces to:
- Merge all pairs with C=1 using DSU.
- For every pair with C=0, their DSU representatives must differ.

This is sufficient because each DSU class can be realized as:
- size 1: single vertex
- size >=2: a cycle through all vertices in the class, giving at least two simple paths between any pair.
Then connect all classes by bridges arbitrarily in a tree.

*/

public class Main {
    static class DSU {
        int[] p, sz;
        DSU(int n) {
            p = new int[n];
            sz = new int[n];
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                p[i] = i;
                sz[i] = 1;
            }
        }
        int find(int x) {
            while (p[x] != x) {
                p[x] = p[p[x]];
                x = p[x];
            }
            return x;
        }
        void union(int a, int b) {
            int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
            if (ra == rb) return;
            if (sz[ra] < sz[rb]) {
                int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t;
            }
            p[rb] = ra;
            sz[ra] += sz[rb];
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        int M = fs.nextInt(); // unused
        int Q = fs.nextInt();

        int[] A = new int[Q];
        int[] B = new int[Q];
        int[] C = new int[Q];

        DSU dsu = new DSU(N);

        for (int i = 0; i < Q; i++) {
            A[i] = fs.nextInt();
            B[i] = fs.nextInt();
            C[i] = fs.nextInt();
            if (C[i] == 1) dsu.union(A[i], B[i]);
        }

        boolean ok = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < Q; i++) {
            if (C[i] == 0 && dsu.find(A[i]) == dsu.find(B[i])) {
                ok = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        System.out.println(ok ? "Yes" : "No");
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do {
                c = read();
            } while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            int sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            int val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }
    }
